Unix utility 'kill'

The Unix utility 'kill' terminates the process identified by the process identification number
given as its only parameter. The identification number of a process can be obtained using the
'ps' or 'top' utilities, or is shown by the shell right after starting a process in the
background with an ampersand character, for example 'clock &'.

If the informed identification number does not match any process currently running on the
system, a message is shown and nothing else happens. Terminating a process this way frees its
memory immediately.

USAGE:

kill <pid>

The <pid> parameter is required and must be the identification number of a process currently
running on the system.

For more information, enter 'kill ?' or 'kill --help'.

